This JavaScript function is supposed to return the maximum sum of any contiguous subarray of length `k`, but it returns a wrong answer for at least one input. Find the bug and provide the corrected code.
function maxWindowSum(arr, k) {
  let maxSum = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i <= arr.length - k; i++) {
    let sum = 0;
    for (let j = i; j < i + k - 1; j++) {
      sum += arr[j];
    }
    maxSum = Math.max(maxSum, sum);
  }
  return maxSum;
}
Trace through `maxWindowSum([1, 2, 3, 4], 2)` step by step to show what it currently returns versus the correct answer, identify the exact off-by-one, and give the fixed function.

The Go program below launches 100 goroutines that each increment a shared counter 1000 times, then prints the final value, expecting 100000. Running it under `go run -race` reports a data race, and the printed value is frequently wrong. Identify exactly which line(s) cause the race and provide a corrected version using a `sync.Mutex` (do not just switch to `atomic.AddInt64` -- explain why the mutex approach is what's being asked for here, then also mention atomic as a one-line alternative).
package main
import (
	"fmt"
	"sync"
)
func main() {
	counter := 0
	var wg sync.WaitGroup
	for i := 0; i < 100; i++ {
		wg.Add(1)
		go func() {
			defer wg.Done()
			for j := 0; j < 1000; j++ {
				counter++
			}
		}()
	}
	wg.Wait()
	fmt.Println(counter)
}

Below is a Python function meant to remove every even number from a list in place and return it. It sometimes leaves an even number behind, especially when there are two even numbers in a row.
def remove_evens(nums):
    for i in range(len(nums)):
        if nums[i] % 2 == 0:
            del nums[i]
    return nums
Explain precisely why deleting from a list while iterating over it by index causes elements to be skipped (walk through `remove_evens([2, 4, 5, 6, 7])` to demonstrate), note that this will also eventually raise an `IndexError` for some inputs, and provide a corrected implementation along with a brief note on which of the common fixes (iterate over a copy, build a new list via comprehension, or iterate backwards) you chose and why.

The following Node.js function is supposed to fetch a user, then fetch that user's orders, and return a combined object. In production it intermittently returns `orders: undefined` even though the orders clearly exist in the database.
async function getUserWithOrders(userId) {
  const user = await fetchUser(userId);
  let orders;
  fetchOrdersForUser(user.id).then(result => {
    orders = result;
  });
  return { user, orders };
}
Identify the missing `await` (or equivalent fix) that causes `orders` to be read before the promise resolves, explain why this bug is intermittent rather than always failing (hint: timing / event loop ordering), and rewrite the function so it reliably waits for both fetches before returning.

This Flask endpoint has a SQL injection vulnerability. Identify exactly how an attacker could exploit it (give a concrete malicious value for the `username` query parameter that would let them bypass the intended filter and dump the whole `users` table), and provide the corrected version using parameterized queries.
@app.route('/users/search')
def search_users():
    username = request.args.get('username', '')
    query = "SELECT id, username, email FROM users WHERE username LIKE '%" + username + "%'"
    cursor.execute(query)
    rows = cursor.fetchall()
    return jsonify(rows)
Also note whether simply escaping quote characters yourself would be a sufficient fix, and explain briefly why parameterized queries are the correct approach instead.

Given these Django models:
class Post(models.Model):
    title = models.CharField(max_length=200)
    author = models.ForeignKey('User', on_delete=models.CASCADE)
    created_at = models.DateTimeField(auto_now_add=True)

class Comment(models.Model):
    post = models.ForeignKey(Post, related_name='comments', on_delete=models.CASCADE)
    author = models.ForeignKey('User', on_delete=models.CASCADE)
    body = models.TextField()

and this view function, which is unacceptably slow on a page listing 50 posts, generating well over 150 database queries per request according to the Django Debug Toolbar:
def post_feed(request):
    posts = Post.objects.all().order_by('-created_at')[:50]
    result = []
    for post in posts:
        comment_count = post.comments.count()
        author_name = post.author.username
        result.append({
            'title': post.title,
            'author': author_name,
            'comment_count': comment_count,
        })
    return JsonResponse({'posts': result})
Explain exactly which lines inside the loop each trigger an additional query per post (there are two separate N+1 sources here, not one), and rewrite `post_feed` using `select_related` and/or `annotate` with `Count` so the whole view executes in a small constant number of queries regardless of how many posts are on the page.

The React-free vanilla JavaScript component below creates a memory leak: every time `openModal()` is called and then the modal is closed and reopened, an additional resize handler accumulates, so eventually the resize callback fires dozens of times per actual resize event and the page gets progressively slower.
function openModal(modalEl) {
  modalEl.classList.add('open');
  window.addEventListener('resize', () => {
    repositionModal(modalEl);
  });
}
function closeModal(modalEl) {
  modalEl.classList.remove('open');
}
Explain precisely why `closeModal` does nothing to undo what `openModal` set up, why the anonymous arrow function used as the listener makes the leak harder to fix with a naive `removeEventListener` call, and provide a corrected pair of functions that don't accumulate listeners across repeated open/close cycles.

The Java code below is meant to transfer money between two `Account` objects safely, but under concurrent load it occasionally deadlocks completely, freezing the two threads involved forever.
class Account {
    private final Object lock = new Object();
    private double balance;
    Object getLock() { return lock; }
    void transfer(Account to, double amount) {
        synchronized (this.getLock()) {
            synchronized (to.getLock()) {
                this.balance -= amount;
                to.balance += amount;
            }
        }
    }
}
Explain the exact interleaving of two threads calling `accountA.transfer(accountB, 50)` and `accountB.transfer(accountA, 30)` simultaneously that produces the deadlock (name which thread holds which lock and what each is waiting for), and provide a corrected `transfer` implementation that establishes a consistent global lock ordering (e.g. by a stable unique account ID) so the deadlock cannot occur regardless of transfer direction.

The C function below is meant to safely detect integer overflow before it happens and return an error code instead of a wrapped-around result, but the overflow check itself is broken because it relies on undefined behavior.
int safe_add(int a, int b, int *result) {
    int sum = a + b;
    if (sum < a || sum < b) {
        return -1; /* overflow detected */
    }
    *result = sum;
    return 0;
}
Explain why, with `a = INT_MAX` and `b = 1`, the expression `a + b` is signed integer overflow, which is undefined behavior in C -- meaning the compiler is free to assume it never happens and may optimize the `if` check away entirely, so this function cannot reliably detect the very overflow it's checking for. Then provide a corrected, fully portable implementation that checks for overflow before performing the addition (e.g. by comparing `b` against `INT_MAX - a`), without ever computing an out-of-range value.

The JavaScript snippet below is supposed to log 0, 1, 2, 3, 4 (one second apart) but instead logs 5 five times.
for (var i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
  setTimeout(function() {
    console.log(i);
  }, i * 1000);
}
Explain precisely why `var`'s function-scoping (as opposed to block-scoping) causes every callback to share the same single `i` variable, which has already reached 5 by the time any callback runs. Then give two different correct fixes: one that changes `var` to `let`, and one that keeps `var` but fixes it using an IIFE that captures the current value of `i` as a parameter -- explain briefly why the `let` fix works in terms of per-iteration binding.

Below is a Python function that silently returns `None` on any failure instead of surfacing what actually went wrong, which has made a production incident nearly impossible to debug from the logs alone.
def load_config(path):
    try:
        with open(path) as f:
            return json.load(f)
    except:
        return None
Explain concretely what kinds of real problems this bare `except:` swallows beyond the intended 'file not found' case (give at least two examples, such as a `KeyboardInterrupt` during a long read, or a `json.JSONDecodeError` from a genuinely malformed config file that a developer needs to know about), and rewrite the function to catch only the specific exceptions that represent recoverable, expected failure modes, while logging enough detail to diagnose the rest, and letting truly unexpected exceptions propagate.

The pagination helper below is meant to return page `page` (1-indexed) of `pageSize` items from a larger `allItems` array, but it silently skips one item at the boundary between every pair of consecutive pages.
function getPage(allItems, page, pageSize) {
  const start = page * pageSize;
  const end = start + pageSize - 1;
  return allItems.slice(start, end);
}
Trace through `getPage(items, 1, 10)` on an array of 30 items to show exactly which items it returns versus which it should return for a correctly 1-indexed page 1, identify the two separate bugs (the `start` calculation and the `end`/slice semantics), and provide the corrected function.

The React component below is meant to poll a `count` value and log its current value once per second, but it always logs 0 no matter how long it runs, even though the displayed count on screen updates correctly via the button.
function Counter() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
  useEffect(() => {
    const id = setInterval(() => {
      console.log('current count:', count);
    }, 1000);
    return () => clearInterval(id);
  }, []);
  return (
    <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>
      Count: {count}
    </button>
  );
}
Explain why the empty dependency array `[]` causes the `setInterval` callback to close over the `count` value from the render when the effect first ran, and never see subsequent updates, then provide a corrected version -- either by adding `count` to the dependency array (and explain the tradeoff of re-creating the interval on every render) or by using a ref to always read the latest count.

The Java singleton implementation below is intended to be a thread-safe, lazily-initialized singleton, but under concurrent access on multiple CPU cores it can occasionally hand out a reference to a partially-constructed `Singleton` object with fields still at their default values.
public class Singleton {
    private static Singleton instance;
    private int configValue;
    private Singleton() {
        configValue = loadExpensiveConfig();
    }
    public static Singleton getInstance() {
        if (instance == null) {
            synchronized (Singleton.class) {
                if (instance == null) {
                    instance = new Singleton();
                }
            }
        }
        return instance;
    }
}
Explain why this double-checked-locking pattern is broken without the `volatile` keyword on `instance` -- specifically, how the JVM's memory model permits instruction reordering during object construction such that another thread can observe a non-null `instance` reference before the constructor has finished writing all of its fields. Provide the one-line fix (`private static volatile Singleton instance;`) and explain briefly why `volatile` alone is sufficient here without needing a full lock on every read.
